URL Shortener
=============

Takes a long URL, returns a short code. Visit the code, get redirected.

Built to learn system design. Each version fixes one problem found by
measuring, not by guessing. The build log below is the actual point of
this repo.

Stack
-----

* Language: Python
* Framework: Flask, served by gunicorn
* Database: PostgreSQL 17
* Cache: Redis 7
* Infrastructure: Docker Compose

Run it
------

1. Start the services:

       docker compose up -d

2. Create the schema:

       docker compose exec -T postgres psql -U mia -d links < schema.sql

3. Install and run:

       python3 -m venv .venv
       source .venv/bin/activate
       pip install -r requirements.txt
       gunicorn -w 4 'v4-app:app' -b 127.0.0.1:5001 --reload

4. Use it:

       curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5001/shorte
         -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
         -d '{"url": "https://example.com"}'

Copy .env.example to .env and set your own value


Build log
=========

v1: it works, in memory
-----------------------

Two endpoints, a Python dict, no database.

Problem: codes could collide. token_urlsafe(4) gives 4 bytes, so
2^32 = 4,294,967,296 possible codes. With 10 mil

       10,000,000 / 4,294,967,296  = 0.23% chanc
       0.23% x 10,000,000          = ~23,000 collisions

23,000 links silently pointing at the wrong site, with no error.

Two options: check before storing, or use longer codes. Longer codes only
reduce the odds. Checking gives a guarantee, and
paying on the write path is cheap.

Fix: retry on collision. The primary key enforces uniqueness, so the
database does the checking.


v2: it survives a restart
-------------------------

Problem: the dict died every time the server res

Fix: SQLite. Code is the primary key, because ev
a code and needs the URL, never the reverse. Long URLs are not unique and
must not be.

Then: was the disk read slow enough to need a ca

Isolated it by adding /ping, a route that touche

                   req/sec    p50
       /ping          856     12ms
       /<code>        817     12ms

4% apart. SQLite was not the bottleneck. The dev

Fix: gunicorn with 4 workers.

                   req/sec    per request
       /ping         7837     1.28ms
       /<code>       6670     1.50ms

9x faster. Not one line of app.py changed. WSGI
so servers swap freely.

Cache decision: no. The database now cost 0.22ms, about 15% of the
request. Not worth another service to run and ke


v3: it can run on more than one machine
---------------------------------------

Problem: SQLite coordinates writers with operati
works when every process asks the same operating system. Put the file on a
network share and two machines each believe they
write. The file corrupts, silently.

       SQLite     many processes, one machine
       Postgres   many processes, many machines

Fix: Postgres. Four changes total, the SQL was u

       sqlite3.connect("links.db")  ->  psycopg.
       ?                            ->  %s
       sqlite3.IntegrityError       ->  psycopg.

Cost of the move:

                   req/sec    per request
       /ping         7855     1.27ms
       /<code>        825    12.13ms

The database went from 0.22ms to 10.9ms. 50 time
query, same logic.

Same question as before: the query, or the connection? psycopg.connect()
was inside the function, so every request paid a
authentication to run one 1ms query.

Fix: a connection pool. Connections open once at startup and get lent out.

                   req/sec    per request
       before        825     12.13ms
       after        3759      2.66ms

4.6x faster, for moving one line out of a function. No new service.


v4: the cache
-------------

The database was now 1.39ms of a 2.66ms request. 52%. Added Redis to
learn the pattern, not because the numbers deman

Cache-aside: check Redis, on a miss query Postgr
The cache knows nothing about the database. The app does the work.

                                      req/sec    per req
       postgres + pool                  3759
       postgres + pool + redis          5386      1.9ms

43% faster, not the 2x expected. Redis is also a separate process reached
over TCP. A 1.39ms Postgres round trip became a
Cheaper, not free. A cache gives you a shorter trip, not memory speed.

Caveat: ab hits one URL repeatedly, so this was a 100% hit rate. Real
traffic spreads across thousands of links, and a
before, because it checks Redis first and still goes to Postgres. 43% is
the ceiling, not the average.

Entries expire after an hour. That is a backstop
how long a stale entry can live. It is not deletion. If a delete endpoint
is added, the key must be removed from Redis at


